[AS7's Example 13.11 (autotransformer)]

e,1,0,(120∠30°)
t,1,2,80,80+120
rl,2,0,8+6j

analysis: ac
note: Refer to the autotransformer circuit. Calculate I1, I2 and Io if ZL = 8 + j6 Ω, and the complex power supplied to the load. There is no autotransformer element; the same t element does it, with the 80 turns of the lower winding and the 80 + 120 of the whole. I1 is it1, the current entering the transformer at node 1 (75.00 at -6.87°); I2 is the opposite of it2 (30.00 at -6.87°); Io is the opposite of their sum, aa(-it1-it2), 45.00 at 173.13°.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/as7e1311.png
omega: omega
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: yes


[The autotransformer as one tapped winding]

e,1,0,(120∠30°)
t,[1,0],[2,1],[80,120]
rl,2,0,8+6j

analysis: ac
note: The same autotransformer described as what it is, one winding with a tap: the lower section, 80 turns, from the tap (node 1) to ground, and the upper section, 120 turns, from the top (node 2) down to the tap. The second winding's bottom is the first winding's top, which is what the four-terminal form is for. In this problem, we describe a transformer with all four terminals for the first time. it1 is the total entering the transformer at node 1, the source current I1, 75.00 at -6.87°; I2 is the opposite of it2, 30.00 at -6.87°. The same answers as the two-node description.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_auto_tapped.png
omega: omega
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: yes


[A transformer whose primary is not grounded]

e,1,0,10
r1,1,2,1
t,[2,3],[4,0],[2,1]
r3,3,0,3
rl,4,0,8

analysis: dc
note: The primary sits between nodes 2 and 3, neither of them ground, so it is written as the pair [2,3]; the secondary's bottom is ground, [4,0]. The 8 ohm load reflects to the primary as 32 ohm, in series with 1 + 3, so the primary current is 5/18 A: it2 = 5/18 entering at node 2, it3 = -5/18 leaving at node 3, and it4 = -5/9 on the secondary, doubled by the 2:1 ratio and negative because it leaves the transformer into the load. irl = 5/9 A.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_t_live_primary.png
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes


[Both windings between live nodes, and why a side needs a ground]

e,1,0,10
r0,1,2,1
t,[2,4],[3,5],[2,1]
r4,4,0,3
rl,3,5,100
r5,5,0,7

analysis: dc
note: Both windings between live nodes: the 100 ohm load sits across the secondary and node 5 is tied to ground through 7 ohm. it2 = 5/202, it4 = -5/202, it3 = -5/101, it5 = 5/101, and ir5 = 0: the secondary's current circulates through the load alone. The resistor is what gives the secondary side a reference -- an ideal transformer conducts nothing from one winding to the other. Delete r5 and Symbulator still solves, taking node 5 as that side's own reference and saying so in a note; the currents and v3 - v5 come out the same.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_t_live_pairs.png
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
evaluate: -se


[NR11's 60 V source and coupled 2 H and 8 H coils (DC, t < 0)]

e,1,0,60
r9,1,a,9
r3,a,2,3
l1,2,0,2
l2,3,4,8
r2,3,5,2
r10,5,4,10
m,l1,l2,2

analysis: dc
note: Nilsson & Riedel (11th ed.): the switch has been at a for a long time and moves to b at t = 0. Find i1 and i2 for t > 0. Before the switch, in DC, the source drives i1 = 60/12 = 5 A through the 9 and 3 ohm and the 2 H coil; the 8 H side carries nothing. Both dots are at the coils' top ends, so each coil's first node is its top.
note: The secondary has no path to node 0 -- a coupling conducts nothing across -- so Symbulator takes node 4 as that side's reference and says so in a note.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_nr11_coupled_60v.png
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes


[NR11's 60 V source and coupled 2 H and 8 H coils (TR, t > 0)]

r3,0,2,3
l1,2,0,2,5
l2,3,4,8,0
r2,3,5,2
r10,5,4,10
m,l1,l2,2

analysis: tr
note: After the switch, the 3 ohm and the 2 H coil are shorted through b and the 5 A decays, coupled by 2 H into the 8 H coil and its 12 ohm. il1 = (5/2)(e^(-t) + e^(-3t)) A and il2 = (5/4)(e^(-t) - e^(-3t)) A: two modes, at 1 and 3 per second, il1 starting from 5 A and il2 from 0. The initial current of l1 is the DC entry's answer.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_nr11_coupled_60v.png
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes
plottool: plot_time
plotkey: i_l1
plotmin: 0
plotmax: 5
plotpoints: 300


[NR11's two switches and coupled 3 H and 15 H coils (DC, t < 0)]

e,a,0,20
r10,a,c,10
l2,d,c,15

analysis: dc
note: Nilsson & Riedel (11th ed.): switch 1 has been open and switch 2 at a for a long time; at t = 0 switch 1 closes and switch 2 moves to b. Find i1 for t > 0. Before the switch there is no primary current at all, and the 20 V drives 2 A through the 10 ohm and down through the 15 H coil, whose dot is at its bottom: its first node is d, the bottom, so the current from d to c reads -2 A.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_nr11_coupled_two_switches.png
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes


[NR11's two switches and coupled 3 H and 15 H coils (TR, t > 0)]

e1,1,0,24
r120,1,2,120
l1,2,0,3,0
l2,d,c,15,-2
r360,c,d,360
m,l1,l2,3

analysis: tr
vars: il1
note: After the switches, the 24 V drives the 3 H primary through 120 ohm from rest, while the 15 H secondary, starting at 2 A, decays through 360 ohm; the coupling is 3 H with the dots at opposite ends, so the secondary is written bottom node first. i1 = 1/5 - (31/20) e^(-20t) + (27/20) e^(-60t) A: it starts from 0, settles at 24/120 = 0.2 A, and the two modes are at 20 and 60 per second.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_nr11_coupled_two_switches.png
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes
plottool: plot_time
plotkey: i_l1
plotmin: 0
plotmax: 0.3
plotpoints: 300


[NR11's 90 V source and coupled 3 H and 2 H coils (DC, t < 0)]

e,1,0,90
r5,1,t,5
l1,t,m,3
l2,m,0,2
r10,m,0,10
m,l1,l2,1

analysis: dc
note: Nilsson & Riedel (11th ed.): the switch has been at a for a long time and moves to b at t = 0. Find io for t > 0. Before the switch both coils are shorts in DC, the 2 H shorting the 10 ohm, so the source drives io = 90/5 = 18 A through the 3 H and on through the 2 H, and the 10 ohm carries nothing.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_nr11_coupled_90v.png
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes


[NR11's 90 V source and coupled 3 H and 2 H coils (TR, t > 0)]

r20,t,0,20
l1,t,m,3,18
l2,m,0,2,18
r10,m,0,10
m,l1,l2,1

analysis: tr
vars: il1
note: After the switch the source is gone and everything decays through the 20 ohm and the 10 ohm; both dots are at the coils' upper ends. io = il1 = 12 e^(-2t) + 6 e^(-20t) A, from 18 A to zero with modes at 2 and 20 per second.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_nr11_coupled_90v.png
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes
plottool: plot_time
plotkey: i_l1
plotmin: 0
plotmax: 2.5
plotpoints: 300


[NR11's capacitor shorted by a switch and coupled 0.8 H and 1.6 H coils (DC, t < 0)]

e,1,0,48
r4,1,3,4
l1,3,0,0.8
l2,4,5,1.6
r20,4,5,20
m,l1,l2,0.8

analysis: dc
note: Nilsson & Riedel (11th ed.): the switch across the 10 mF capacitor has been closed for a long time and opens at t = 0. Find vo, across the 20 ohm on the secondary, for t > 0. Before the switch the capacitor is shorted, so it is left out of the DC picture: the 48 V drives 12 A through the 4 ohm and the 0.8 H coil, and the secondary carries nothing.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_nr11_coupled_capacitor.png
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes


[NR11's capacitor shorted by a switch and coupled 0.8 H and 1.6 H coils (TR, t > 0)]

e,1,0,48
c,1,2,10'm,0
r4,2,3,4
l1,3,0,0.8,12
l2,4,5,1.6,0
r20,4,5,20
m,l1,l2,0.8

analysis: tr
vars: v4, v5, il1
note: After the switch opens the capacitor enters the primary loop, uncharged, with the coil carrying its 12 A; the coupling is 0.8 H with both dots at the top. The Evaluate line reads vo = v4 - v5 = e^(-5t)(60 cos 10t - 120 sin 10t) - 60 e^(-25t) V: a damped oscillation at 10 rad/s on the primary's series RLC, plus the secondary's own mode at 25 per second, starting from 0 and returning to 0.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_nr11_coupled_capacitor.png
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
evaluate: v4-v5
plottool: plot_time
plotkey: v_4
plotmin: 0
plotmax: 1
plotpoints: 300


[NR12's Problem 7.68]

e,1,0,200
r50,1,2,50
l1,2,3,4,0
l2,0,3,8,0
m,l1,l2,5

analysis: tr
vars: il1, v2, v3
note: Nilsson & Riedel (12th ed.), Problem 7.68: no energy stored when the switch closes at t = 0. Find i, v1 and v2 for t > 0. The 4 H coil's dot is at its left end, node 2, and the 8 H coil's at its bottom, node 0, so the 8 H is written bottom node first; the current enters one dot and leaves through the other, and the pair is 4 + 8 - 10 = 2 H. i = il1 = 4(1 - e^(-25t)) A, v1 = v2 - v3 = -100 e^(-25t) V and v2 = v3 = 300 e^(-25t) V: v1 runs against the rise because the 5 H coupling outweighs the 4 H, and the two still add to 200 - 50 i.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_nr12_p0768.png
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes
evaluate: v2-v3
plottool: plot_time
plotkey: i_l1
plotmin: 0
plotmax: 0.2
plotpoints: 300


[NR12's Problem 7.70]

e,1,0,10
r250,1,a,250
l1,a,0,0.5,0
l2,0,a,0.25,0
m,l1,l2,0.25

analysis: tr
note: Nilsson & Riedel (12th ed.), Problem 7.70: no energy stored when the switch closes. Find io, vo, i1 and i2 for t > 0. The 0.5 H coil's dot is at its top and the 0.25 H coil's at its bottom, so the second is written bottom node first: the parallel pair opposes and reduces to (0.125 - 0.0625)/(0.75 + 0.5) = 0.05 H, tau = 0.2 ms. io = ir250 = 0.04(1 - e^(-5000t)) A, vo = va = 10 e^(-5000t) V, i1 = il1 = 0.016(1 - e^(-5000t)) A and i2, downward, is -il2 = 0.024(1 - e^(-5000t)) A.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_nr12_p0770.png
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes
evaluate: -il2
plottool: plot_time
plotkey: i_r250
plotmin: 0
plotmax: 0.001
plotpoints: 300


[NR12's Problem 7.71]

e,1,0,15
r75,1,a,75
l1,a,0,8'm,0
l2,a,0,20'm,0
m,l1,l2,10'm

analysis: tr
note: Nilsson & Riedel (12th ed.), Problem 7.71: no energy stored when the switch closes. Find io, vo, i1 and i2 for t > 0. Both dots are at the top, so both coils are written top node first: the parallel pair aids and reduces to (160 - 100)/(28 - 20) = 7.5 mH, tau = 0.1 ms. io = ir75 = 0.2(1 - e^(-10000t)) A, vo = va = 15 e^(-10000t) V, i1 = il1 = 0.25(1 - e^(-10000t)) A and i2 = il2 = -0.05(1 - e^(-10000t)) A -- backwards, because with both coils shorts at the end the split is set by the flux linkages, and 0.25 - 0.05 is the 0.2 A the 75 ohm allows.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/sym_nr12_p0771.png
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes
plottool: plot_time
plotkey: i_r75
plotmin: 0
plotmax: 0.0005
plotpoints: 300
